"In Lesson 1 you worked eight switches by hand. Every lesson since then has been about switches too: switches wired into gates, gates into an adder, adders into a processor, and programs telling the processor what to do. One question is left. How did a machine made of switches shrink from the size of a room to something you carry in your pocket? The first switches were relays. A relay is a switch moved by an electromagnet, so one electric current can turn another on and off. In 1937 George Stibitz built an adder from just two of them. But a relay has parts that move, and moving parts are slow. The next switch had no moving parts at all: the vacuum tube. ENIAC, finished early in 1946, used more than 17,000 vacuum tubes. It was far faster than any relay machine: it could do up to 5,000 additions a second. It was also enormous. It filled a basement 50 feet long and 30 feet wide, and it gave off so much heat that it needed its own air conditioning. Then, in December 1947, two researchers at Bell Labs made a switch inside a small piece of solid material. It was called a transistor. Nothing in it moved and nothing had to glow hot. Eleven years later came the next step: the integrated circuit, in which many parts are made together from one piece of material. We call it a chip. From then on, the story is one of fitting more switches onto a chip. In 1971 the Intel 4004 put a whole processor on one chip, with 2,300 transistors. In 2024 Apple announced a chip with 28 billion. In 1965 Gordon Moore had noticed how quickly the number was growing; ten years later he described it as doubling every two years. That pattern became known as Moore’s law. So the answer is simple to say and astonishing to think about. The idea never changed: a switch is off or on, 0 or 1. The switch itself changed, from a clicking relay to something far too small to see. Everything in these sixteen lessons, from adding two numbers to opening a web page, rests on that one idea, repeated an enormous number of times.": "a825c938179d",
